# =============================================================================
# DeepSeek-V4-Flash MXFP4 at 262144 -- kvram placement + MTP speculative decoding
# =============================================================================
# LEGACY: kvram + MTP at 262144; will not convert (RESULTS 27.4)
# The generation-first sibling of `deepseek-v4-flash-256k-kvram`, and the place
# where MTP pays best on this box.
#
#   MEASURED at 130k depth, caches on, 160 generated tokens (RESULTS §15):
#     256k kvram profile (n18)      406.1 pp / 16.31 tg
#     n20, no MTP                   376.9 pp / 15.47 tg
#     this profile (n20 + MTP)      364.2 pp / 20.48 tg
#   MTP alone is worth +32.4 % (15.47 -> 20.48); net against the shipped 256k
#   profile that is +25.6 % generation for -10.3 % prefill.
#
# That is a better trade than the same treatment at 131072 (+20.4 % isolated,
# +13.5 % net, §14.1) — deeper context leaves generation more bandwidth-starved,
# so drafted tokens save proportionally more.
#
# Why n20: the 5.5 GiB predictor needs VRAM the n18 placement does not have, and
# at this context the compute buffer is 2.8 GiB rather than 1.8, so it costs two
# steps. Verified from the load log that the target split is identical with and
# without the draft (82 838.93 / 66 290.00 MiB) — the draft accounts for the
# whole 7.2 GiB VRAM difference, so the comparison isolates MTP.
#
# NOT a quality trade: every drafted token is verified against the target model.
#
#   ./serve.sh deepseek-v4-flash-256k-kvram-mtp
#   ./serve-deepseek-v4-flash-mxfp4-kvram-mtp-256k.sh
#
# Same robustness caveats as every kvram profile: manual placement, and -rtr
# forces --no-mmap so each start re-reads the model.
#
# Requires the predictor (5.5 GiB): philpax/DeepSeek-V4-Flash-MTP-Only-GGUF
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

: "${IK_MODEL_GLOB:=${IK_MODELS_ROOT}/lmstudio-community/DeepSeek-V4-Flash-0731-GGUF/DeepSeek-V4-Flash-0731-MXFP4-00001-of-*.gguf}"

: "${IK_ALIAS:=deepseek-v4-flash-256k-kvram-mtp}"
: "${IK_CTX:=262144}"

: "${IK_FIT:=0}"
: "${IK_NCMOE:=20}"

# -rtr STAYS ON HERE, and this was tested rather than assumed (RESULTS §27.4).
# Converting to the gpu-experts principle -- experts computed on the GPU, see §21 --
# does not fit: the draft model holds 5.5 GB of weights plus its own KV cache
# (-cd 8192) in VRAM, so --n-cpu-moe 21 at -ub 8192 will not load and n20 at
# -ub 4096 loads and then dies at depth in cuMemCreate. Both CUDA OOM, not the NaN
# abort.
#
# It would be the wrong trade anyway. This profile measures 27.01 tg at 4k and
# 26.51 at 32k against 20.29 for the plain kvram profile -- +33 %, the highest
# generation recorded on this model, which is the entire reason it exists.
: "${IK_RTR:=1}"
: "${IK_UBATCH:=2048}"
: "${IK_BATCH:=4096}"
: "${IK_CTK:=f16}"
: "${IK_CTV:=f16}"

: "${IK_MTP_DRAFT:=${IK_MODELS_ROOT}/philpax/DeepSeek-V4-Flash-MTP-Only-GGUF/DeepSeek-V4-Flash-MTP-bf16.gguf}"
: "${IK_EXTRA_ARGS:=-mla 3 -fidx --reasoning-format deepseek -nkvo -md ${IK_MTP_DRAFT} -ngld 99 -cd 8192 --spec-type mtp:n_max=1,p_min=0.0}"
